# Written 2026-10-18, before `git init`, so that whenever this does become a
# repository the private material is already excluded rather than excluded
# after the fact. Git history is effectively permanent: a file removed in a
# later commit stays readable at its old commit URL, in every clone and in
# every fork. The only reliable fix is to never commit it.
#
# The posture here is default-deny. Everything that has not been read and
# cleared for publication is ignored; un-ignoring is a decision, dropping
# something quietly is not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- runtime state
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
bc2serve_state/
results/
bc2serve.env
*.log
*.pid
*.sif
.DS_Store

# ------------------------------------------------------- internal notes, private
# Decided by the user on 2026-10-18: the context files stay private. Extended to
# every top-level document, because all but two name the lab's machines,
# accounts, filesystem paths or queues, and one holds unpublished results.
# A README written for release is the exception and is un-ignored below.
/*.md
!/README.md

# ----------------------------------------------- not yet cleared for publication
# (Patterns that would themselves name the site live in .git/info/exclude.)
# Default-deny, not a decision. Each of these names one site's hosts, queue names or
# home paths. Review them one by one at release time and un-ignore what should
# ship.
/batch/
/bindcraft2-image/
/deploy/
/docs-tutorial/
*.command
# The installer the binderscope README tells Mac users to double-click; scanned, clean.
!/binderscope/Install Binderscope.command

# Files that carry a hostname or a home path. Ignored rather than scrubbed,
# because neither is needed to run or understand the program.
#
# (A trailing comment on a pattern line is part of the pattern, so these stand
#  alone. The first version of this block had them inline and so ignored
#  nothing at all — caught only by running `git add` and scanning what staged.
#  Scan what stages, not what you meant to ignore.)
#
# start-jupyter.sh: a fully qualified host name and an account.
start-jupyter.sh
# binderscope/scripts/: one-off analysis scripts with absolute result paths stay
# out. The four that the package and its tests need are named and ship: without
# validate_schema.py a strict build refuses to run, and the code and docs send
# people to fetch_reference.py and the two guide builders. Each was scanned for
# hosts, paths, accounts and sequences before it was un-ignored.
/binderscope/scripts/*
!/binderscope/scripts/validate_schema.py
!/binderscope/scripts/fetch_reference.py
!/binderscope/scripts/build_guide.py
!/binderscope/scripts/guide_to_md.py
# make_demo.py turns a bundle into the public demo; it holds no data of its own.
!/binderscope/scripts/make_demo.py

# A note on the files that are NOT ignored here, because they must ship.
#
# An earlier version of this file declared the package documentation clean. It
# was not. The check had looked for host names and paths and found none, while
# several documents, a few source comments and two tests reported measurements
# from unpublished campaigns. Clean of machine identifiers is not the same as
# clean of private data, and the second kind does not announce itself: most of
# those passages never named a campaign, they said "on the real campaign" and
# then gave the number.
#
# Those passages were rewritten on 2026-10-18 to keep the reasoning and drop
# the measurement, and the tests that embedded real values now use synthetic
# ones. If you add a document, a docstring or a fixture, the standing rule is
# that a number may ship only if it came from a toy fixture, a published
# source, or the code itself.

# The public demo is a build of make_demo.py; it lives on the gh-pages branch, not here.
/demo/
